~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
[EXPLAIN] What this step does
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
  Step 01 produced one error value (MAE, in pIC50 units) for every test fold, for each way of
  describing the molecules         (Morgan fingerprints vs. RDKit 2D + 3D descriptors +
  fingerprints). This step asks a single question: is the difference         between those two
  descriptions real, or could it be just luck of how the compounds were split into folds?
  The test used is a paired t-test with the Nadeau-Bengio correction. A plain t-test would be
  over-confident here because         the folds share most of their training compounds (they are
  not independent); the correction widens the uncertainty to         compensate.
  How to read the result: p < 0.05 means the two descriptions truly differ in accuracy; p >= 0.05
  means we cannot claim         one is better than the other, and the simpler/cheaper one (Morgan)
  is then the reasonable choice.
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
